Instruction:
can you please create a python script that outputs a derivative using The Average Rate of Change Formula A(x) = f(b)-f(a) /(b-a) when x is user input

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\ Sure! Here's a Python script that accomplishes the task:

```python
def derivative(x):
    a = float(input("Enter a: "))
    b = float(input("Enter b: "))
    f_a = float(input("Enter f(a): "))
    f_b = float(input("Enter f(b): "))
    A = (f_b - f_a) / (b - a)
    return A

x = float(input("Enter x: "))
derivative_value = derivative(x)
print("The derivative of the function at x =", x, "is", derivative_value)
```

This script takes user input for the values of a, b, f(a), and f(b) and calculates the derivative using the Average Rate of Change Formula. The user is prompted to enter these values at runtime. The resulting derivative is then printed to the console.

